{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["Ba gad — 'good fortune has come to us,' like ״הַעֹרְכִים לְגַד שֻׁלְחָן״. And likewise in tractate Shabbat: ״גד גדי וסינוק לא״. And likewise in Bereshit Rabbah: ״אֲתָא מַזְלָא דְּבֵיתָא״. And the one who explains it as the language of גדוד (raiding band) is mistaken, for even though the names are patterned after the utterance — as with Reuven: ״כִּי רָאָה יְהֹוָה בְּעָנְיִי״; Shimon: ״כִּי שָׁמַע יְהֹוָה״ — the woman's utterance we write only in the manner of the core of the statement, and גד cannot be the language of גדוד without a doubled letter of two dalets. ״יָגֹדּוּ עַל נֶפֶשׁ צַדִּיק״ — the dagesh is under the second dalet that is missing. Like ״וַיָּכֹּו אֶת עֲוֺנָם״, ״וְיָסֹלּוּ עָלַי אֹרְחוֹת אֵידָם״, ״יָסֹבּוּ עָלַי רֵבִים״ — in all of them a dagesh in place of the missing letter."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {0 "Rashbam's grammatical argument: forms like יגדו display a dagesh in the remaining consonant compensating for a dropped nun or second root letter; therefore גד as a noun cannot derive from גדוד, which would require a geminated dalet. The lemma and biblical quotations retain their Hebrew forms per protocol."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:45:48.515587983Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}